  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThis strategy redefines the problem as a convex optimization task, leveraging the fact that the overlap integral for each lag is a linear function of h. By introducing a new variable t and enforcing constraints that each overlap integral is \u2264 t, we formulate a convex problem that can be solved globally with a convex optimizer like ECOS. This approach guarantees finding the global minimum, avoiding suboptimal results from local methods used previously. The key bottleneck addressed is the non-convex nature of the original formulation, which is now transformed into a convex problem using constraints for all lags.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables: \n- h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points} (continuous variables for h[i])\n- t \u2208 \u211d (new variable to represent the maximum overlap)\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i (enforces h in [0,1])\n- sum(h) = n_points / 2 (exact integral constraint)\n- For each lag k in [0, 2n_points - 2], compute the overlap integral as a linear expression in h and add the constraint overlap_integral_k \u2264 t\n\nObjective: \n- Minimize t\n\nDerivation:\nThe overlap integral for lag k is \u222b h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx. For a discrete approximation with step size dx = 2/n_points, this is approximated as a sum of h[i] * (1 - h[j]) * dx, where j = i + k for i within bounds. Each lag's contribution is linear in h, and the maximum over all lags is enforced by adding constraints for each lag.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInputs: \n- n_points (number of samples)\n- initial_h_values (optional starting point)\n\nSteps:\na. Check if initial_h_values exist and resample if necessary\nb. Define h as a CVXPY variable and t as an optimization variable\nc. Precompute all pairwise products h[i] * (1 - h[j]) for all i, j to build the overlap expressions for all lags\nd. For each lag k, compute the overlap integral as a linear combination of h[i] and add constraint: overlap_integral_k \u2264 t\ne. Solve the convex optimization problem using ECOS or SCS to find the minimal t and corresponding h\nf. Validate that h satisfies all constraints and compute the final C5 bound using the original evaluator\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault settings:\n- n_points = 256 for better resolution\n- Use ECOS solver with default tolerances\n- If initial_h_values provided, resample to match n_points using linear interpolation\n\nVariations:\n- If no parent provided, start with random binary pattern\n- If solver fails, revert to previous method with nudged initial guess\n- For faster computation, use smaller n_points (e.g., 128) with careful scaling\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime estimation: \n- Precompute overhead: negligible\n- Solver execution: roughly O(n_points^2) in time complexity\n- 256 points: ~256\u00b2 = 65536 operations, within 600-second budget\n\nSafety measures:\n- Track best solution found during iterations\n- On solver failure, fallback to initial pattern and re-solve\n- If no improvement for 30 seconds, terminate and return best found\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nPost-solution checks:\n- Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- Verify sum(h) \u2248 n_points / 2 with absolute error < 1e-6\n- Recalculate C5 with original evaluator to match exact metric\n\nSanity checks:\n- Check that maximum lag value exceeds all other lags\n- Verify no division by zero or invalid indices\n- Ensure returned h is a NumPy array of correct shape and type",
